win32u: SetActiveWindow() on a WS_DISABLED window activates its last active popup, not the window

Premiere Pro, New Project dialog: click away to another app via the dock, click back, and the
dialog "grabs focus then loses focus". Every other cycle. Operator's narrowing: foregrounding
another app from the dock "primes it to throw out the focus it just received".

Captured 2026-09-15 (premiere-20260915-134050.log, WINEDEBUG=+win,+waylanddrv, 11.10-89).
Dialog 0x1a026a is a #32770 owned by the main frame 0x40194; the frame is WS_DISABLED for the
dialog's whole life (NtUserEnableWindow(0x40194,0) at 67275, re-enabled at 103774 after the
dialog closed at 104057). After every CLEAN return, Premiere's PlugPlug (CEP host) runs a
create/destroy cycle and then "restores" activation to the frame:

    13c8 WIN_CreateWindowEx L"PLUGPLUG_UI_NATIVE_WINDOW_CLASS_NAME" ... parent=0x40194   (0x802ce, hidden 1x1)
    13c8 WIN_CreateWindowEx L"06e2f54a-..." style=82000000 0x0                          (0x70294, CEP host popup)
    0298 NtUserSetFocus 0x202e8 -> set_active_window hwnd 0x70294                        (CEPHtmlEngine child)
    13c8 user_destroy_window (0x70294)
    13c8 activate_other_window win = 0x1a026a fg = 0x70294                              <- correct: dialog again
    13c8 user_destroy_window (0x802ce)
    13c8 NtUserSetActiveWindow 0x40194                                                   <- the mover
    13c8 set_active_window hwnd 0x40194, previous 0x1a026a, mouse 0, focus 1

No activate_other_window line precedes that NtUserSetActiveWindow; it is the application's own
SetActiveWindow(main frame). Wine accepts it, the disabled frame becomes the active and
foreground window behind its modal dialog, and the driver then reads fg=0x40194 at the next
leave ("SKIP (not the foreground window)") and at the next return ("SKIP -- foreground 0x40194
is already ours"): the dialog's focus is never re-established. Cycles 1/3/5 clean, 2/4 broken,
matching the log exactly (82751, 92910, 101746). activate_other_window() and its three guarded
callers are upstream-identical and behave correctly here; they were the prior suspect.

Windows: "A window must be enabled before it can be activated" (EnableWindow, Remarks). Wine
already applies that rule to its own automatic re-activation (can_activate_window rejects
WS_DISABLED) and to NtUserSetFocus (WS_DISABLED on the top-level -> fail), but
NtUserSetActiveWindow activated any top-level handle. Upstream-unmodified in our tree.

Fix: in NtUserSetActiveWindow, a WS_DISABLED top-level target is redirected to its last active
popup (GetLastActivePopup; the server's make_window_active keeps it for every owner) when that
popup is visible, top-level, not minimized and enabled; when it is already active nothing
happens and it is returned as "previous"; when there is no activatable popup the call is
refused (returns 0, activation left alone). Enabled targets, child targets and all internal
set_active_window() callers (set_foreground_window, SetFocus, SetWindowPos activation,
activate_other_window) are untouched. In the log's case the redirect target is the dialog,
already active: the call becomes a no-op and fg stays on the dialog.

Kill switch NEUTRON_SETACTIVE_DISABLED=0 restores the upstream behaviour without a rebuild;
NEUTRON_WL_DIAG=1 prints one neutron-setactive-disabled line per redirect/refusal, at the
outcome. Compiled on both arches. Not a workaround for Adobe: PlugPlug's call is legal on
Windows because Windows never lets it activate the disabled frame.

Aside (untouched): set_active_window() returns an uninitialised `ret` when hwnd is already
active (goto done before ret is assigned; gcc -Wmaybe-uninitialized at input.c:2102). Upstream
too.

--- a/dlls/win32u/input.c
+++ b/dlls/win32u/input.c
@@ -2102,12 +2102,64 @@
     return ret;
 }
 
+/* NEUTRON: SetActiveWindow() on a WS_DISABLED top-level window.
+ *
+ * Windows: "A window must be enabled before it can be activated" (EnableWindow, Remarks). Wine
+ * encodes the same rule for its own automatic re-activation (can_activate_window in window.c
+ * rejects WS_DISABLED) and for SetFocus (the WS_DISABLED check above), but NtUserSetActiveWindow
+ * took any top-level handle and made it active. That is how the owner of a modal dialog -- disabled
+ * by the dialog for exactly this reason -- became the active and foreground window while its
+ * dialog was still up: Premiere's PlugPlug (CEP host) tears down a temporary owned window and then
+ * calls SetActiveWindow(main frame) to "restore" activation; the main frame is WS_DISABLED behind
+ * the New Project dialog. From then on the driver saw the frame as foreground, so the next
+ * keyboard-focus leave and return were classified as in-app focus changes and the dialog lost the
+ * focus it had just been given (premiere-20260915-134050.log, +win,+waylanddrv).
+ *
+ * Fix: a WS_DISABLED target activates its last active popup instead (what Windows does when a
+ * disabled owner is asked to take activation), or is refused when there is no activatable popup.
+ * Enabled windows, child windows and the internal set_active_window() callers are untouched.
+ *
+ * Kill switch NEUTRON_SETACTIVE_DISABLED=0 restores the upstream behaviour without a rebuild;
+ * NEUTRON_WL_DIAG=1 reports each redirect or refusal, at the outcome. */
+static BOOL neutron_setactive_disabled_on(void)
+{
+    static int on = -1;
+
+    if (on < 0)
+    {
+        const char *e = getenv( "NEUTRON_SETACTIVE_DISABLED" );
+        on = e ? (e[0] != '0') : 1;
+        ERR( "neutron-gateresolved: NEUTRON_SETACTIVE_DISABLED = %d (%s)\n", on, e ? "environment" : "default" );
+    }
+    return on;
+}
+
+static BOOL neutron_wl_diag(void)
+{
+    static int v = -1;
+    if (v < 0) { const char *e = getenv( "NEUTRON_WL_DIAG" ); v = (e && e[0] == '1') ? 1 : 0; }
+    return v;
+}
+
+/* same test as can_activate_window() in window.c: visible, top-level, not minimized, enabled */
+static BOOL neutron_activatable( HWND hwnd )
+{
+    DWORD style;
+
+    if (!hwnd || !is_window( hwnd )) return FALSE;
+    style = get_window_long( hwnd, GWL_STYLE );
+    if (!(style & WS_VISIBLE)) return FALSE;
+    if ((style & (WS_POPUP|WS_CHILD)) == WS_CHILD) return FALSE;
+    if (style & WS_MINIMIZE) return FALSE;
+    return !(style & WS_DISABLED);
+}
+
 /**********************************************************************
  *           NtUserSetActiveWindow    (win32u.@)
  */
 HWND WINAPI NtUserSetActiveWindow( HWND hwnd )
 {
-    HWND prev;
+    HWND prev, neutron_disabled = 0;
 
     TRACE( "%p\n", hwnd );
 
@@ -2125,9 +2177,40 @@
         style = get_window_long( hwnd, GWL_STYLE );
         if ((style & (WS_POPUP|WS_CHILD)) == WS_CHILD)
             return get_active_window();  /* Windows doesn't seem to return an error here */
+
+        if ((style & WS_DISABLED) && neutron_setactive_disabled_on())
+        {
+            HWND popup = NtUserGetLastActivePopup( hwnd ), active = get_active_window();
+
+            if (popup == hwnd || !neutron_activatable( popup ))
+            {
+                if (neutron_wl_diag())
+                    ERR( "neutron-setactive-disabled: hwnd=%p is WS_DISABLED and its last active popup %p cannot take activation -> REFUSED, active stays %p\n",
+                         hwnd, popup, active );
+                return 0;
+            }
+            if (popup == active)
+            {
+                if (neutron_wl_diag())
+                    ERR( "neutron-setactive-disabled: hwnd=%p is WS_DISABLED -> its last active popup %p is already active, left alone\n",
+                         hwnd, popup );
+                return active;
+            }
+            neutron_disabled = hwnd;
+            hwnd = popup;
+        }
     }
 
-    if (!set_active_window( hwnd, &prev, FALSE, TRUE, 0 )) return 0;
+    if (!set_active_window( hwnd, &prev, FALSE, TRUE, 0 ))
+    {
+        if (neutron_disabled && neutron_wl_diag())
+            ERR( "neutron-setactive-disabled: hwnd=%p is WS_DISABLED -> activating its last active popup %p FAILED\n",
+                 neutron_disabled, hwnd );
+        return 0;
+    }
+    if (neutron_disabled && neutron_wl_diag())
+        ERR( "neutron-setactive-disabled: hwnd=%p is WS_DISABLED -> activated its last active popup %p instead (previous %p)\n",
+             neutron_disabled, hwnd, prev );
     return prev;
 }
 
